---
title: Genomic Read Summary
sidebar:
  order: 11
---

Summary statistics for raw sequence reads in FASTQ format. For assembled contigs, see [Genomic Contig Summary](/docs/api-usage/python/contig_summary/).

## Example

```python
import pysegul

def summarize_reads():
    summary = pysegul.ReadSummary(
        input_fmt='auto',
        mode='complete',
        output_path='tests/output',
        output_prefix='read_summary',
        )
    outputs = summary.from_dir('tests/raw-data')
```

To use a list of files, call `from_files` instead of `from_dir`:

```python
summary.from_files(['tests/raw-data/read1.fastq', 'tests/raw-data/read2.fastq'])
```

## Options

| Option | Default | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `input_fmt` | Required | `'auto'`, `'fastq'`, `'gzip'` |
| `mode` | Required | `'minimal'`, `'basic'`, or `'complete'` (`'default'` accepted as an alias for `'basic'`). See [Summary modes](#summary-modes) |
| `output_path` | Required | Output directory |
| `output_prefix` | `None` | Name of the output files |
| `phred` *(beta)* | `'auto'` | Quality score offset: `'auto'`, `'33'`, `'64'` |
| `dup_sketch_size` *(beta)* | `100000` | Distinct sequences kept to estimate duplication in `complete` mode |
| `plot` *(beta)* | `None` | Chart options. See [Charts and Reports](/docs/api-usage/python/plots_reports/) |
| `qc_profile` *(beta)* | `'auto'` | QC profile: `'auto'`, `'short-wgs'`, `'amplicon'`, `'long-ont'`, `'long-hifi'` |
| `checksum` | `None` | SHA-256 checksum mode in `report.json`: `'none'`, `'outputs'`, `'inputs'`, `'all'`, or `None` (`SEGUL_CHECKSUM`) |

## Methods

| Method | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `from_dir(input_dir)` | Summarize the read files in a directory |
| `from_files(input_files)` | Summarize a list of read files |

Both return a dictionary of output paths: `summary`, `positions`, `distributions`, `report`, and `plots`. `positions`, `distributions`, and full per-sample profiles/QC inside `report.json` are written in `complete` mode only.

## Summary modes

| Mode | Output |
| --- | --- |
| `'minimal'` | Read count only and provenance `report.json` |
| `'basic'` | Per-file and per-read statistics in `read-summary.csv` plus provenance `report.json`. See the [CLI guideline](/docs/cli-usage/read-summary/#statistics) |
| `'complete'` | Basic statistics, plus per-position and relative-position profiles (`read-pos-summary.zip`), distributions (`read-dist-summary.zip`), paired-end and cohort summaries, and full statistics in `report.json` |

Complete mode covers the read quality checks of [FastQC](https://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/projects/fastqc/), [fastp](https://github.com/OpenGene/fastp), [NanoPlot](https://github.com/wdecoster/NanoPlot), and [SeqKit](https://bioinf.shenwei.me/seqkit/), and adds multi-sample and error-probability diagnostics unique to SEGUL. See [how SEGUL compares](/docs/cli-usage/read-summary/#comparison-with-other-tools).

```python
summary = pysegul.ReadSummary(
    'auto',
    'complete',
    'tests/output',
    'read_summary',
    phred='33',
    plot=pysegul.PlotOptions(format='svg'),
    )
outputs = summary.from_dir('tests/raw-data')
```

## Quality checks

{% aside type="caution" title="Beta" %}
This function is in beta and may still change.
{% /aside %}

Load the JSON report with `load_read_report`. Each sample has its statistics, the read quality checks (`qc`), and the worst status among them (`qc_status`: `'Pass'`, `'Warn'`, or `'Fail'`). These are the same checks the GUI and the `segul view summary` viewer show.

```python
report = pysegul.load_read_report(outputs['report'])

for sample in report['samples']:
    metrics = sample['metrics']
    print(sample['sample'], metrics['read_count'], sample['qc_status'])
    for check in sample['qc']:
        print('  ', check['name'], round(check['value'], 1), check['unit'], check['status'])
```
